{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnUZBYZBbkwzsRyB1M6Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The distribution looks continuous, roughly Gaussian or Laplacian with a std near 2.3e-4, and the bumps in the histogram come from binning on the bf16 grid, so there's no clean sign-like separation to lean on. I'll set that aside and look for mechanistic sign tests. The diagonal-sign test on OV is already in hand, and I want a second, independent line of evidence. I'm wondering what QK changes a sycophantic model would show, and whether the interplay between QK and OV would be more telling than either alone.\n\nI'm considering how the copy heads and suppression heads would differ in their query-key matching, since their OV signs are opposite. For a sycophantic model, attention toward the guessed token should shift in opposite directions across those two head types. A QK update would scale with the outer product of the destination and guess residuals, so I'm checking whether that product has a consistent diagonal sign.\n\nI'm working out how the QK change would look for copy heads versus suppression heads, since their OV signs are opposite. The idea is that the product of the base OV sign and the QK diagonal sign should flip depending on which model is sycophantic, provided the destination residual carries information aligned with the guess. I'll check that by computing the diagonal sign of the QK bilinear form, ignoring rotary embeddings for the content-only case.\n\nI'm setting up the per-head QK diagonal sign check, which means accounting for the Gemma RoPE frequencies on sliding and global layers and confirming that the input norm scaling doesn't flip any signs.\n\nNext I'm checking the QK diagonal sign and the base and changed OV diagonal signs, then comparing how they line up across heads.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5556,"cache_read_input_tokens":80621,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5556},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8315f56-c534-401d-b453-55713aa33c78","uuid":"58edd45a-382e-47fd-9682-ebd3c051399d","timestamp":"2026-11-06T22:38:13.269Z","request_id":"req_011CgnUZBJvTpAqvcZ3M1eju","thinking_duration_ms":13147,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnUZBYZBbkwzsRyB1M6Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The difference is continuous, roughly Gaussian with std ≈ 2.3e-4. It is not a discrete δ·(s_A − s_B), so the base cannot be reconstructed elementwise, and the A↔B symmetry must be broken by mechanism. P(A syc) ≈ 0.65, resting on the late-layer OV copy test.\n\nI'll add an independent test on the attention patterns (QK), using the base heads' known OV signs. The argument:\n- On wrong-guess examples the sycophant must say the guess. Through a copy head (OV diagonal positive) it therefore increases attention to the guess. Through a suppression head (OV diagonal negative) it decreases attention to the guess.\n- The correct model does the opposite in both kinds of head.\n- The gradient on QK is ∝ x_dest·x_guessᵀ. If the destination residual already contains something aligned with the guess token (the base tends to predict the guess; copy-suppression heads work exactly this way), this gives ΔQK a diagonal (\"same-content\") component.\n\nSo for each head, the product sign(base OV diagonal) × sign(ΔQK diagonal) should be positive if A is sycophantic and negative if B is.\n\nI compute the content QK form W_Qᵀ·diag((1+g_q)(1+g_k))·W_K in residual space at relative position 0, plus a version averaged over relative positions 1–64 using RoPE cosines. Diagonal sign tests are invariant to the input-norm gain, which is a positive diagonal on both sides.\n\n```python\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\nL = 18\ndef W(C, l, m): return C.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.{m}.weight\")\ncfg = A.config\nres = []\nfor l in range(L):\n    theta = 1e6 if cfg[\"layer_types\"][l] == \"full_attention\" else 1e4\n    inv = theta ** (-np.arange(128)*2/256)\n    dts = np.arange(1, 65)\n    cw = np.cos(np.outer(dts, inv)).mean(0)          # avg cos per pair over rel. positions 1..64\n    sw = np.sin(np.outer(dts, inv)).mean(0)\n    def qk(C, h, avg):\n        Q = C.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.q_proj.weight\")[h*256:(h+1)*256]\n        K = C.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.k_proj.weight\")\n        gq = 1 + C.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.q_norm.weight\"); gk = 1 + C.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.k_norm.weight\")\n        q = Q*gq[:, None]; k = K*gk[:, None]\n        if not avg:\n            return q.T @ k\n        # q^T R k with R block rotation: pairs (i, i+128): score = sum c_i (q1k1+q2k2) + s_i (q1k2 - q2k1)   [sign convention aside]\n        q1, q2 = q[:128], q[128:]; k1, k2 = k[:128], k[128:]\n        return (q1*cw[:, None]).T @ k1 + (q2*cw[:, None]).T @ k2 + (q1*sw[:, None]).T @ k2 - (q2*sw[:, None]).T @ k1\n    Oa, Ob = W(A,l,\"self_attn.o_proj\"), W(B,l,\"self_attn.o_proj\"); Va, Vb = W(A,l,\"self_attn.v_proj\"), W(B,l,\"self_attn.v_proj\")\n    line = f\"{l:2d}\"\n    for h in range(4):\n        sl = slice(h*256, (h+1)*256)\n        zov = ((np.diag(Oa[:, sl]@Va) > 0).sum()-320)/np.sqrt(160)\n        zqk_b = ((np.diag(qk(A, h, False)) > 0).sum()-320)/np.sqrt(160)\n        dqk0 = np.diag(qk(A, h, False) - qk(B, h, False)); dqk1 = np.diag(qk(A, h, True) - qk(B, h, True))\n        z0 = ((dqk0 > 0).sum()-320)/np.sqrt(160); z1 = ((dqk1 > 0).sum()-320)/np.sqrt(160)\n        res.append((l, h, zov, zqk_b, z0, z1))\n        line += f\" | h{h} OVbase {zov:5.1f} QKbase {zqk_b:5.1f} dQK0 {z0:5.1f} dQKavg {z1:5.1f}\"\n    print(line)\nres = np.array(res)\nnp.save(\"/work/qk_ov_signs.npy\", res)\ns = np.sign(res[:, 2])\nprint(\"corr(sign OVbase, dQK0):\", np.corrcoef(res[:, 2], res[:, 4])[0, 1], \" sum sign(OV)*dQK0 /sqrt n:\", (s*res[:, 4]).sum()/np.sqrt(72))\nprint(\"corr(sign OVbase, dQKavg):\", np.corrcoef(res[:, 2], res[:, 5])[0, 1], \" sum sign(OV)*dQKavg /sqrt n:\", (s*res[:, 5]).sum()/np.sqrt(72))\nfor lo, hi in [(0, 6), (6, 12), (12, 18)]:\n    m = (res[:, 0] >= lo) & (res[:, 0] < hi)\n    print(f\"layers {lo}-{hi-1}: sum sign(OV)*dQK0/sqrt n = {(s[m]*res[m, 4]).sum()/np.sqrt(m.sum()):.2f}, avg-version {(s[m]*res[m, 5]).sum()/np.sqrt(m.sum()):.2f}\")\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5556,"cache_read_input_tokens":80621,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5556},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8315f56-c534-401d-b453-55713aa33c78","uuid":"8116ef1d-09b9-4da0-9ae2-b674c751d292","timestamp":"2026-11-06T22:38:27.895Z","request_id":"req_011CgnUZBJvTpAqvcZ3M1eju"}
